---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowRight, ArrowUpRight, WavesHorizontal as Waves, Wind, Activity, Info } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="ENSO & Atlantic Hurricanes" active="none" description="How ENSO changes Atlantic hurricane environments, why central/eastern Pacific tendencies differ, and why seasonal activity cannot determine an individual landfall.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">PACIFIC SIGNAL · ATLANTIC RESPONSE</span><h1>One ocean's signal.<br /><span>Another ocean's season.</span></h1><p>How ENSO can shape the environment for Atlantic hurricanes.</p></div></div>
    <section class="connection-flow" aria-label="El Niño can increase Atlantic wind shear and make hurricane development less favorable">
      <div><Waves size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">TROPICAL PACIFIC</span><h2>El Niño</h2><p>A shift in ocean and atmospheric conditions.</p></div>
      <ArrowRight class="flow-arrow" size={25} />
      <div><Wind size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">ATLANTIC ENVIRONMENT</span><h2>More wind shear</h2><p>Often stronger changes in wind with height.</p></div>
      <ArrowRight class="flow-arrow" size={25} />
      <div><Activity size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">HURRICANE DEVELOPMENT</span><h2>Less favorable</h2><p>A broad tendency, not a storm prediction.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="explanation-grid">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">THE CONNECTION</span><h2>Wind shear can disrupt a storm.</h2><p>El Niño often favors increased vertical wind shear over parts of the Atlantic hurricane development region. La Niña can favor reduced shear. Other ingredients, including Atlantic ocean temperatures and moisture, also matter.</p></div>
      <div><span class="eyebrow">THE LIMIT</span><h2>Fewer storms does not mean no risk.</h2><p>ENSO does not determine the track, strength, or landfall of an individual storm. A quieter basin-wide season can still produce a destructive hurricane.</p></div>
    </section>
    <section class="method-section"><h2>Which basin and which question?</h2><div class="probability-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="ENSO hurricane tendencies" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Useful interpretation</th><th scope="col">Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Atlantic seasonal environment</th><td>El Niño often increases shear; La Niña often reduces it.</td><td>Atlantic SST, moisture and other circulation also matter.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Central/eastern North Pacific activity</th><td>El Niño often favors activity; the Atlantic tendency cannot be applied to every Pacific basin.</td><td>Use a basin-specific seasonal assessment.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Individual storm or landfall</th><td>Read NHC advisories and local warnings.</td><td>No ENSO phase provides a track or landfall probability for one storm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="body-copy">La Niña is not a sufficient condition for a hyperactive Atlantic season. Nor does an El Niño year imply that the coast is safe. Basin-wide accumulated activity and the risk at a single location are different quantities: the steering pattern and the storm’s evolution must be assessed separately.</p><p class="body-copy">Mechanism source: <a href="https://www.climate.gov/news-features/blogs/enso/impacts-el-nino-and-la-nina-hurricane-season">NOAA ENSO Blog, May 30, 2014</a>, checked November 13, 2026. This archived article is used for mechanisms and competing drivers; its 2014 seasonal forecast is not current guidance. See the <a href="/faq/#hurricane-impact">concise FAQ answer</a> for the same boundaries.</p></section>
    <div class="content-notice"><Info size={19} /><div><strong>Use official storm information for decisions.</strong><p>This page describes climate context. It does not provide warnings, evacuation advice, or a current seasonal hurricane forecast.</p></div></div>
    <div class="source-callout"><Waves size={22} /><div><strong>Follow the current Atlantic storm picture.</strong><p>Current advisories and warnings are published by the National Hurricane Center.</p></div><a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open NHC <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div>
  </div>
</Layout>
